# The NIO `Path`

Modern file I/O = **`java.nio.file`** (Java 7+). Old `java.io.File` is retired. Two types: **`Path`** (a location) · **`Files`** (operations).

## A `Path` is *just an address*

Building one touches no disk; the file need not exist.

```java
Path p = Path.of("/home/ada/notes.txt");   // Java 11+
Path r = Path.of("docs", "report.pdf");     // joined with OS separator
```

## Combine & inspect (still no disk)

```java
base.resolve("notes.txt");   // join a child   /home/ada/notes.txt
base.relativize(file);       // route between  notes.txt
p.getFileName();  p.getParent();
p.normalize();               // a/./b/../c → a/c  (defuses ../ traversal)
```

Prefer `resolve` to gluing strings with `"/"`.

## Why over old `File`

- **Honest errors** — `Files` ops throw `IOException`; old `File` returned `false`
- Symlinks, attributes, atomic moves — features `File` lacked

**`Path` = address you build offline; `Files` = where it meets the disk.**
